.TH KINTSUGI-COMMIT 1 "2026-10-22" "kintsugi 0.8.0" "Kintsugi Manual"
.SH NAME
kintsugi-commit \- split the working tree into atomic commits
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kintsugi commit
.RB [ \-\-apply ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Read the working tree, split unrelated changes into atomic commit groups ordered
by dependency (source before the tests and docs that describe it), and generate a
message per group from the diff.
.PP
Dry-run is the default and needs no trust: nothing is staged, nothing is
committed, and git is not written to. \fB--apply\fR is the deliberate step; it
asks for confirmation first. Nothing here ever pushes \- publishing stays the
operator's own decision.
.SH OPTIONS
.IP \fB\-\-apply\fR
Run git commit per group after confirmation.
.IP \fB\-\-json\fR
Print the plan as JSON instead of human-readable text.
.IP \fB\-\-yes\fR
Skip the confirmation prompt (only meaningful with \fB--apply\fR).
.SH EXAMPLES
Preview the commit groups:
.PP
  $ kintsugi commit
.PP
Commit them:
.PP
  $ kintsugi commit --apply
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kintsugi (1),
.BR kintsugi-review (1)
